          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Which drug classes does heart failure guidance describe for reduced ejection fraction?",
          "answer": "The latest guidelines for heart failure with reduced ejection fraction recommend four foundational drug classes. These include:\n\n1. **ACE Inhibitors** - Angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors are commonly prescribed to help relax blood vessels and lower blood pressure.\n\n2. **ARBs** - Angiotensin II receptor blockers are alternatives for patients who cannot tolerate ACE inhibitors.\n\n3. **Beta-blockers** - These help slow the heart rate and reduce blood pressure, improving heart function over time.\n\n4. **Mineralocorticoid receptor antagonists** - These are used to reduce the effects of aldosterone, which can lead to fluid retention and hypertension.\n\nAdditionally, SGLT-2 inhibitors are also noted for their cardiovascular benefits in patients with heart failure, especially those with comorbid diabetes (source: \"Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction: the four foundational therapy classes\").\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
